WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.140
<v Examiner>Four marks.

2
00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:06.054
<v Examiner>Describe the difference between type 1 and type 2 diabetes.

3
00:00:06.054 --> 00:00:10.498
<v Examiner>Then explain how rheumatic fever develops, who it affects, and why.

4
00:00:10.948 --> 00:00:15.914
Diabetes is a long-term condition where the body can't control its blood sugar,

5
00:00:15.914 --> 00:00:19.308
either because it doesn't make enough insulin, or any,

6
00:00:19.308 --> 00:00:22.703
or because the cells have become resistant to insulin.

7
00:00:23.153 --> 00:00:27.748
Type 1 is autoimmune: the body destroys its own insulin-making cells,

8
00:00:27.748 --> 00:00:30.612
and it's the type usually seen in children.

9
00:00:30.612 --> 00:00:36.007
The classic symptoms: frequent urination, polyuria; increased thirst, polydipsia;

10
00:00:36.007 --> 00:00:41.669
extreme hunger, polyphagia; plus unintended weight loss, blurry vision and tiredness.

11
00:00:41.669 --> 00:00:45.998
Type 2 is insulin resistance, from genetic and lifestyle factors.

12
00:00:45.998 --> 00:00:51.926
It can have the same symptoms, plus recurrent infections and sores that are slow to heal.

13
00:00:51.926 --> 00:00:56.055
The numbers: normal blood glucose is four to seven point five.

14
00:00:56.055 --> 00:00:57.454
A hypo is under four.

15
00:00:57.454 --> 00:01:00.651
Hyperglycaemia is over seven point five fasting,

16
00:01:00.651 --> 00:01:03.848
or over eleven point one two hours after a meal.

17
00:01:03.848 --> 00:01:08.111
A person with glucose of thirty, ketones and vomiting has D K A.

18
00:01:08.561 --> 00:01:13.795
Rheumatic fever is a serious autoimmune disease that can follow a strep throat that

19
00:01:13.795 --> 00:01:16.507
wasn't treated, or wasn't treated properly.

20
00:01:16.507 --> 00:01:22.183
Up to five weeks later, symptoms appear: fever, aching joints, tiredness, jerky movements,

21
00:01:22.183 --> 00:01:23.381
and heart problems.

22
00:01:23.381 --> 00:01:28.742
Its dangerous complication is rheumatic heart disease, which can be life-threatening.

23
00:01:29.192 --> 00:01:29.957
Who and why.

24
00:01:29.957 --> 00:01:32.573
It mostly affects children and teenagers,

25
00:01:32.573 --> 00:01:36.208
with much higher rates in Māori and Pacific young people.

26
00:01:36.208 --> 00:01:38.441
The why is the social determinants:

27
00:01:38.441 --> 00:01:43.799
crowded living conditions and large group settings like schools spread strep throat,

28
00:01:43.799 --> 00:01:48.073
and the barriers to seeing a doctor mean sore throats go untreated.

29
00:01:48.073 --> 00:01:51.262
Treatment is bed rest, pain relief and penicillin,

30
00:01:51.262 --> 00:01:54.515
then penicillin injections every twenty-eight days,

31
00:01:54.515 --> 00:01:58.406
with extra antibiotic cover for dental visits and procedures.

32
00:01:58.856 --> 00:02:04.797
Type 1 diabetes: autoimmune, children, no insulin; polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia,

33
00:02:04.797 --> 00:02:05.646
weight loss.

34
00:02:05.646 --> 00:02:11.446
Type 2: insulin resistance, lifestyle and genes, plus infections and slow healing.

35
00:02:11.446 --> 00:02:14.982
Glucose four to seven point five; hypo under four.

36
00:02:14.982 --> 00:02:21.065
Rheumatic fever: autoimmune, after untreated strep throat; Māori and Pacific children,

37
00:02:21.065 --> 00:02:26.299
crowded housing; penicillin every twenty-eight days; can damage the heart.
